"""kms-play-py - full screen KMS player with three hardware planes.

============================================================================
ARCHITECTURE
============================================================================
The display is composed by the Rockchip VOP from three DRM planes, bottom to
top:

    video plane   NV15 (10-bit) or NV12 (8-bit), fed by kmssink directly from
                  the decoder's DMA-BUFs: zero copy, no CPU conversion. When no
                  hardware decoder can negotiate with kmssink (H.264 here), a
                  second tier replays the file with software decoders through
                  videoconvert, one attempt only (see DECODE TIERS).
    "sub" plane   ARGB8888 subtitles, pre-multiplied alpha, blended by the VOP.
    "osd" plane   ARGB8888 OSD (progress bar, volume, track labels).

Only the two text planes are ever written by the CPU, and they are small bands
(a few hundred rows), so the video never travels through system memory.

Process layout, and why:

    kms-play-py (this file, unprivileged user)
      |- kms-subd   owns the DRM master, drives the two ARGB planes, serves its
      |             DRM fd over a unix socket with SCM_RIGHTS.
      |- kms-keys   reads every /dev/input/event*, prints "key NAME 0|1|2".
      |- gst pipeline (playbin -> kmssink), a child of this process.

The player itself has no DRM master: libkmsshare.so (LD_PRELOAD, set by the
kms-play wrapper) intercepts its open("/dev/dri/card*") and hands it a copy of
kms-subd's fd. That is also why the VT switch must happen BEFORE drmSetMaster
(see switch_vt): the VT that currently owns the console keeps the master
locked, and the ioctl fails with EACCES.

============================================================================
DECODE TIERS
============================================================================
Tier 1 is playbin's own choice: the best-ranked decoder, its DMABufs going
straight to kmssink. If that errors (typically not-negotiated, a hardware
decoder that cannot hand its buffers to kmssink) or never reaches PLAYING,
tier 2 rebuilds a playbin with force-sw-decoders, hardware decoders demoted
to rank NONE and "videoconvert ! kmssink", then seeks back to the position
reached. One attempt: a second failure stops with a clear message.

============================================================================
STARTUP BANNER
============================================================================
Once the decode tier and the video caps are known, the OSD plane shows a
four-line banner in the top left corner: decode tier, codec/profile/resolution/
frame rate, video plane format and memory, and the active connector and mode.
It stays for 8 s, then fades out over 0.8 s (12 renders: the only animation in
the player) and the plane is cleared. Any key cancels it immediately and frees
the plane for the usual bottom band. Both surfaces are written to
/dev/shm/kms-osd-<uid>.raw together with a .geom file holding "WxH", because
kms-shot cannot otherwise tell which geometry a dump contains.

============================================================================
KEYBOARD MODEL
============================================================================
kms-keys emits a press (1), a release (0) and, while a key is held, repeats
(2) that it generates itself. This file treats press and repeat as "taps" for
the seek keys, so holding an arrow walks up the seek scale exactly like
pressing it repeatedly. All discrete actions (A, S, SPACE, ...) only react to
value 1, so a held key cannot toggle something in a loop.

============================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE IS *NOT*
============================================================================
It does not see the screen either. Every visual claim has to be backed by
~/.cache/kms-play/last.log or by kms-planes output.
"""

